Have you created any Ingress resources, what ingress controller you created
			While a Kubernetes Ingress Resource is just a configuration file containing routing rules (like a blueprint), 
			the Ingress Controller is the actual active daemon running inside the cluster that reads those rules and dynamically forwards traffic to the correct Pods. 
			It acts as a single, smart "front door" for your microservices, eliminating the need to spin up expensive individual cloud load balancers for every service
			
			
Types of Ingress Controllers

There are dozens of third-party ingress controllers. They generally fall into two main architectural categories:

1. In-Cluster (Open-Source / Proxy-Based)

These run as regular Pods inside your cluster and rely on an underlying open-source reverse proxy software. They typically require a single external network load balancer (Layer 4) to route outside traffic to them.
• NGINX Ingress Controller: The most popular and officially maintained option by the Kubernetes community.
• Traefik: A dynamic, cloud-native proxy that automates certificate management and routing natively.
• HAProxy: Known for high performance, advanced routing algorithms, and security mitigation.
• Envoy-Based Gateways (Emissary, Contour, Gloo): High-performance proxies geared toward service meshes and modern API gateway architectures.

2. Cloud-Native (Managed Cloud Load Balancers)

These are built by public cloud providers and integrate directly with their proprietary Layer-7 hardware/software load balancers. Instead of running proxy pods inside the cluster, the controller configures the cloud's infrastructure natively.
• AWS ALB Ingress Controller: Spins up and configures Amazon Application Load Balancers.
• GCP Ingress (GLBC): Deploys Google Cloud HTTP(S) Load Balancers.
• Azure AGIC: Links directly to Azure Application Gateway.

How to Create / Deploy an Ingress Setup

Setting up Ingress requires two steps: deploying the controller software, and then applying your routing rules. Below is a standard walkthrough using the community NGINX Ingress Controller.

Step 1: Install the Ingress Controller

The standard way to install a controller is via Helm. Run these commands in your terminal:
bash
# Add the official NGINX Ingress repository
helm repo add ingress-nginx https://github.io
helm repo update

# Install the controller into its own namespace
helm install my-ingress ingress-nginx/ingress-nginx \
  --namespace ingress-nginx \
  --create-namespace
Use code with caution.
This spins up the NGINX proxy pods and provision a public cloud IP address to capture external traffic.

Step 2: Define and Apply Your Ingress Rules

Once the controller is active, you create an Ingress resource YAML to tell the controller where to route web requests.
Create a file named my-routing.yaml:
yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: app-ingress
  annotations:
    # Tells Kubernetes which controller should process this resource
    kubernetes.io/ingress.class: "nginx"
spec:
  rules:
  - host: myapp.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /api
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: api-service
            port:
              number: 8080
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: web-frontend-service
            port:
              number: 80
Use code with caution.
Apply the file to your cluster:
bash
kubectl apply -f my-routing.yaml
Use code with caution.
How it routes traffic:
• External requests hitting ://example.com are automatically directed to your internal backend microservice (api-service).
• All other traffic (://example.com) is funneled directly to your frontend microservice (web-frontend-service).

			
How a traffic will be moving or how communication happens in cluster with all ingress/egress services
				
	Domain name/Ip/Url > Loadbalancer > Ingress controller(rules) > Kubernets service (Internal load balancer, fixed cluster IP within cluster) > healthy Pods (based on label, namespace rueles etc) 			
				
1. Ingress Traffic (External → Inside Cluster)
Ingress manages how external users or internet traffic reach applications inside the cluster.
• DNS Resolution: A user visits a domain name, which resolves to an external cloud load balancer or IP.
• Cloud/Node Load Balancer: The request hits the cloud provider' s load balancer (such as AWS ALB or GCP Load Balancer).
• Ingress Controller: The load balancer hands traffic to an Ingress Controller (like NGINX, Traefik, or Envoy), which reads defined routing rules (hostnames, URI paths, SSL/TLS termination).
• Kubernetes Service: The Ingress Controller forwards the request to an internal Kubernetes Service. The Service acts as a stable internal load balancer with a fixed ClusterIP and DNS name.
• Application Pod: The Service load-balances the request across healthy, ephemeral application Pods.

2. East-West Traffic (Pod → Pod Inside Cluster)
Internal communication between microservices happens over a flat network where every Pod gets a unique IP address.
• Service Discovery: Pod A wants to talk to Pod B. Instead of hardcoding Pod B's changing IP, Pod A calls Pod B’s Kubernetes Service DNS name.
• kube-proxy / CNI: The cluster's Container Network Interface (CNI) plugin and kube-proxy route the packet directly to the destination Pod's IP without Network Address Translation (NAT).
• Network Policies (Microsegmentation): By default, all Pods can talk to each other freely. If Network Policies are active, the cluster inspects pod selectors, namespace selectors, or port rules to allow or block the traffic.

3. Egress Traffic (Inside Cluster → External)
Egress defines how internal Pods reach outside systems, such as third-party APIs, external databases, or payment gateways.
• Direct Outbound: By default, Pods can initiate outbound connections straight to the external internet or corporate network.
• Egress Gateways / Proxies: In hardened clusters, outbound traffic is forced through an Egress Gateway, a forward proxy, or a Network Address Translation (NAT) gateway.
• Egress Network Policies / Firewalls: Security teams apply egress rules (using CIDR IP blocks or external FQDN allowlists) to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration or restrict which external APIs a Pod can touch.	


	
