How you will improve performance of a Jenkins job

You can improve Jenkins job performance by "offloading execution to agents", "running steps in parallel", and "optimizing caching and triggers".
1. Offload Execution to Build Agents
• Move away from the Master Node: Never run heavy build tasks on the Jenkins master/controller node; reserve it purely for scheduling and coordinating.
• Use Distributed Agents: Run jobs on dedicated static or dynamic cloud/Docker/Kubernetes agents so multiple builds execute independently without resource contention. Learn more about setting up agents via Master Distributed Builds, Agent Scaling & Speed Optimization.

2. Optimize Pipeline and Job Design
• Run Tasks in Parallel: Use parallel blocks in Jenkins Pipelines to execute independent steps (like multi-platform testing or microservice builds) simultaneously rather than sequentially.
• Split Long Jobs: Break monolithic, multi-hour jobs into smaller, modular pipeline tasks or downstream jobs.
• Use Pipelines as Code: Transition from traditional freestyle jobs to declarative or scripted Pipelines stored directly in version control. Review guidelines on Jenkins Best Practices.

3. Implement Caching and Workspace Management
• Cache Dependencies: Persist and reuse dependency directories (such as node_modules, Maven/Gradle local repositories, or Docker image layers) across builds instead of downloading them from scratch every time.
• Clean Workspaces Wisely: Avoid full, time-consuming workspace wipes on every run; instead, clean up targeted build artifacts or use shared/persistent volumes where supported.
• Limit Build History: Retain only a sensible number of past builds and artifacts to prevent disk I/O bottlenecks and excessive disk usage.

4. Tune Triggers and Polling
• Replace Polling with Webhooks: Stop using heavy Poll SCM cron schedules that constantly hammer your version control system. Switch to push-based Webhooks so jobs trigger immediately only when code actually changes. Watch a breakdown on Mastering Jenkins - Build Triggers.
• Use H Jitter Syntax: Stagger scheduled jobs using Jenkins H (hash) syntax to prevent scheduled spikes from overwhelming the controller.

5. Audit Controller and JVM Health
• Uninstall Unused Plugins: Regularly audit and remove old or inactive plugins that add memory overhead and slow down startup times.
• Upgrade and Tune JVM: Run a modern Long-Term Support (LTS) release of Jenkins, configure proper fixed heap sizes (e.g., using G1GC garbage collection parameters), and back up configuration files cleanly. Explore server tuning tips on Jenkins Performance Best Practices.



If a conflict happened on cicd, how you will resolve
				
Step 1: Identify the Root Cause from Pipeline Logs
• Check the failure stage: Open your CI/CD dashboard (such as GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, or Jenkins) and review the execution logs to pinpoint whether the failure happened during source control (merge/rebase), build compilation, automated testing, or deployment.
• Isolate the commit: Look at the latest pull request or commit diff that triggered the run to see which files or dependencies were modified.

Step 2: Resolve Source Code & Merge Conflicts
If the pipeline fails due to overlapping code changes or merge conflicts:
• Pull and rebase locally: Fetch the latest target branch (e.g., main or develop) into your local feature branch.bash
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main
Use code with caution.
• Fix conflict markers: Open the conflicting files in an editor like VS Code Source Control Docs, review the <<<<<<< HEAD, =======, and >>>>>>> blocks, and manually select or combine the required logic.
• Stage and continue: Mark the files as resolved and continue or commit the merge:bash
git add <resolved-file>
git rebase --continue
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Address Build and Dependency Conflicts
If the conflict involves package versions, runtime environments, or build scripts:
• Lock dependencies: Ensure dependency lock files (such as package-lock.json, yarn.lock, or Pipfile.lock) are synced and committed properly so all pipeline runners use identical package trees.
• Check environment parity: Verify that local and CI/CD environment variables, Node/Python/Java runtimes, or Docker image tags match. Use containerized builds or Dockerfiles to eliminate "works on my machine" discrepancies.
• Use automated dependency tools: Integrate automated tools like Dependabot or Renovate to catch and test version clashes in isolation before they hit the main branch.

Step 4: Validate and Re-run
• Run local pre-commit hooks: Use tools like Husky or local lint/test suites to verify the fix before pushing.
• Push the fix and monitor: Push your resolved commit to trigger a fresh CI/CD pipeline run and confirm that build, test, and deployment stages turn green.
• Enforce branch protections: Set up required pull request reviews, status checks, and merge queues in your repository settings to prevent concurrent code collisions in the future.
		

